From c408ccd398e9efd30a3a7b8724dda7394704a596 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Stockdale <john@offbyone.example>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 20:26:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio_hw: fail adev_open when the devices factory cannot open
 a device

adev_open() logs openDevice() failures but then populates the ops
table and returns 0 with adev->deviceIface left null. The caller
(e.g. pulseaudio-modules-droid) sees a successful open, logs
"Opened hw audio device", and the first forwarded call - typically
init_check() - dereferences the null sp<DeviceHalInterface> and
crashes the client:

  signal=11 SEGV_MAPERR fault_addr=0x0
  PC = audio.hidl_compat.default.so+0x2280
  (adev_init_check trampoline: ldr x8,[x0] with
   x0 = adev->deviceIface = null; LR = PC-8, the return from ALOGV)

Observed on Ubuntu Touch 24.04 / halium-13.0 (Samsung Tab S9 Ultra):
any condition that makes the vendor HAL refuse the open (here
Samsung's vendor.audio.use.primary.default fallback latch returning
ENODEV through the factory) became a PulseAudio SIGSEGV crash loop
instead of a clean module-load failure, masking the real error.

Propagate the error and free the wrapper so clients get a failed
open and fall back gracefully. Same code is present on halium-12.0
through halium-16.0 and master; the patch applies to all.
---
 audio/audio_hw.cpp | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/audio/audio_hw.cpp b/audio/audio_hw.cpp
index 341119d..86ad05d 100644
--- a/audio/audio_hw.cpp
+++ b/audio/audio_hw.cpp
@@ -676,6 +676,9 @@ static int adev_open(const hw_module_t* module, const char* name,
     int rc = devicesFactoryHal->openDevice(AUDIO_HARDWARE_MODULE_ID_PRIMARY, &adev->deviceIface);
     if (rc) {
         ALOGE("devicesFactoryHal->openDevice() error %d loading module %s", rc, name);
+        delete adev;
+        *device = nullptr;
+        return rc < 0 ? rc : -ENODEV;
     }
 
     adev->hw_device.common.tag = HARDWARE_DEVICE_TAG;
